# ===========================================================================
# STAGE #24 - The sliver at 4 <= |nu| <= 8
# ===========================================================================
#
# Why a whole band of Bessel parameter space had no usable method, and how measuring the instrument first changed the answer.
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It does not depend on, and does not
# refer you to, any other notebook. Everything needed to reproduce, run,
# view and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# WHAT THIS STAGE WAS ABOUT
#
# The crate evaluates Bessel functions of COMPLEX ORDER by offering several
# different expansions and picking whichever reports the smallest error
# estimate. Each expansion is valid somewhere and useless elsewhere:
#
#   * the ascending series          - good for small |z|, loses digits as
#                                     exp(|z|) grows
#   * the 1/z asymptotic expansions - good for large |z|, needs |4 nu^2|
#                                     small compared with |z|
#   * the Debye expansion           - a large-order expansion
#   * the Airy-type expansion       - valid near the turning point |z| ~ |nu|
#
# The task was a band where NONE of them would accept: |nu| between about 4
# and 8, with |z| a few times larger. On a map of (order, argument ratio)
# it is a thin diagonal strip, which is why it was called a 'sliver'.
#
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY FOUND
#
# Measuring first, before writing any new expansion, found three things:
#
#  1. THE MEASURING INSTRUMENT WAS BROKEN. Accuracy at complex order had
#     been judged by the J-Y Wronskian
#
#         J_(nu+1) Y_nu - J_nu Y_(nu+1) = 2 / (pi z)
#
#     divided by its own largest term. Off the real axis this is vacuous:
#     J and Y are both (H1 +- H2)/2, so when one Hankel function dominates
#     the other they are THE SAME FUNCTION to within |H1/H2|, and the
#     scaled residual reports that ratio rather than any error. At
#     nu = 5+2i, z = 200+80i it read 8.2e-24 - which looked like a triumph
#     and was simply |H1|/|H2|.
#
#  2. THREE BRANCH DEFECTS in the Debye route, each returning a confident,
#     plausible, wrong number:
#       - the square root t = (1 - x^2)^(1/2) taken on the principal
#         branch, whose CUT IS the oscillatory region the expansion covers;
#         crossing it exchanges the two solutions, so H1 came back as H2 -
#         wrong by a factor of 2e23;
#       - the same defect at REAL order as soon as z is complex;
#       - the prefactor (2 pi nu t)^(-1/2), a SEPARATE branch, crossed once
#         arg z > pi/2, negating both members. A Wronskian is bilinear, so
#         it is blind to a sign shared by both - that defect survived every
#         check in the crate by construction.
#
#  3. THE GUARD WAS WRITTEN IN THE WRONG VARIABLE. It refused the region by
#     ORDER (|nu| >= 8) when accuracy is actually governed by |z|/|nu| and
#     arg(z/nu). It was simultaneously too strict (refusing good small-order
#     points) and far too loose (accepting large-order points whose error
#     estimate was 1e14 times too small).
#
# The sliver went from 94% to 97% served - not by adding a method, but by
# repairing the branches and restating the guard in the measured variables.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is posim notebook source. Lines beginning with '#' are
# comments and are ignored by the interpreter; every other line is a command
# that will be executed in order.
#
# From the repository root (the directory holding Cargo.toml), run ONE of:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook StageNbks/Stage_24.posim
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --script   StageNbks/Stage_24.posim
#
#   --notebook  prints an In[n]:= / Out[n]= transcript, like a Mathematica
#               notebook. This is the mode to use when reading along.
#   --script    prints the same transcript; the two differ only in how the
#               prompt lines are laid out. Either is reproducible.
#
# To work through it by hand instead, start the interactive interpreter:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release
#
# and paste the command lines one at a time. Type HELP for the full command
# language, and %quit to leave.
#
# First run will compile the workspace (a few minutes). Later runs are
# immediate. If 'cargo' is not found, install Rust from https://rustup.rs .
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BROWSER GUI, AND WHY IT IS NOT USED BY THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# posim has a live 3-D scene window served over HTTP and viewed in a browser.
# THIS NOTEBOOK DOES NOT OPEN IT, and you should not try to, because it would
# be empty. The reason is exact:
#
#   The window renders SIMULATOR BODIES - points, spheres, cuboids, tori,
#   disks, cylinders, dumbbells - by sending their positions each frame.
#   This notebook creates none. Running 'scene create' here prints
#
#       showing 0 entities
#
#   and 'scene start' then advances a world containing nothing: the Start,
#   Pause, Stop and Reverse buttons all respond, and nothing moves, because
#   there is nothing to move.
#
# That is not a defect to be worked around. The subject of this notebook has
# no position and no time coordinate, so there is nothing for a renderer to
# draw. Its results are the numbers and messages printed by the commands
# below, and those ARE the output.
#
# IF YOU WANT A WORKING SCENE WINDOW, use a notebook that builds bodies -
# StageNbks/Stage_2C.posim (six bodies in a box) and
# StageNbks/Stage_2H.posim (Newton's cradle and a settling ball) both open
# one automatically and start it running. The complete window procedure is
# written out inside each of those files.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANIMATION: NOT POSSIBLE HERE, AND THE EXACT REASON WHY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This stage cannot be animated, and the cause is precise rather than a
# matter of effort:
#
#   The scene window renders SIMULATOR ENTITIES. Concretely, it draws the
#   bodies held in the PhysicalObjectSystem - points, spheres, cuboids,
#   tori, disks, cylinders and dumbbells - by reading their positions and
#   orientations each frame and sending them over a websocket.
#
#   This stage evaluates SPECIAL FUNCTIONS. A call such as
#   bessel_j_nu(0.5, 2.0) creates no body, has no position, and has no time
#   coordinate: it is a pure function of its arguments. There is therefore
#   nothing for the renderer to draw, and 'scene create' on this session
#   would open a window showing an empty grid and the world axes.
#
#   That is not a defect and there is no workaround inside the scene window.
#   A function of a complex variable is not a moving object.
#
# What you CAN watch instead, and how, is written in the section above: the
# numbers printed by the command lines below change as you vary the order
# and the argument, and that variation IS the phenomenon this stage is about.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The commands below evaluate Bessel functions across the band in question.
# Watch how the values behave as the order and argument ratio change.
#
# A real order and a real argument, well inside the easy region: every
# method agrees here and the answer is machine-precision.
bessel_j_nu(5.0, 15.0)
bessel_y_nu(5.0, 15.0)

# Now walk the argument ratio z/nu across the sliver at nu = 5.
# 1/z asymptotics need |4 nu^2| = 100 small compared with |z|, so they
# only become available once |z| is comfortably past 12.5.
bessel_j_nu(5.0, 6.0)
bessel_j_nu(5.0, 10.0)
bessel_j_nu(5.0, 20.0)
bessel_j_nu(5.0, 40.0)

# The same walk for Y, which is the harder of the pair because it is built
# from a reflection that cancels.
bessel_y_nu(5.0, 6.0)
bessel_y_nu(5.0, 10.0)
bessel_y_nu(5.0, 20.0)

# The identity that adjudicates all of this. For any order and argument,
#     J_(nu+1) Y_nu - J_nu Y_(nu+1) = 2 / (pi z)
# The right-hand side involves no Bessel function at all, which is what
# makes it a usable referee - PROVIDED you remember the resolution limit
# described above. At real order and real argument it is trustworthy.
bessel_j_nu(6.0, 20.0) * bessel_y_nu(5.0, 20.0) - bessel_j_nu(5.0, 20.0) * bessel_y_nu(6.0, 20.0)
2 / (3.14159265358979 * 20.0)

# Those two lines should agree to about fifteen digits. If you change 20.0
# to 60.0 in all four places they still agree; that is the check the stage
# used, and the point of the stage is that the SCALED version of it stops
# working off the real axis while looking like it still works.

# ===========================================================================
# END OF STAGE #24 NOTEBOOK
# ===========================================================================
